WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.850
I have no idea what’s going to happen next.

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:08.290
Normally, when I do a video, I have a script
and a plan.

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:10.980
But this time, no such luck.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:16.240
This is the high voltage test lab
at the University of Manchester

00:00:16.259 --> 00:00:19.390
and the idea is that we’re going to hit
one of those

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:22.600
with more than a million volts
from one of these.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.660
I… I should probably get back down.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.300
This is the UK’s largest
multipurpose high-voltage lab.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.200
We basically test, design and do research
on all kinds of electrical assets.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.300
We also do use the lab for teaching.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.550
We have an impulse generator that can
go up to 2 million volts.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:43.280
Historically this lab would have been used
to actually test

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.860
how lightning affects an aircraft,

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.420
but we’ve never tested a drone before.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.820
And a drone's actually made of 
composite materials, plastics.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.890
Is it going to fry the electricals?

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:53.370
Is it going to fry the battery?

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.110
Or is it going to be fine?

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.910
&gt;&gt; What you think's going to happen?

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.440
I think it will blow up.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.020
I probably shouldn’t say that, should I?

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.380
This is the kind of capacitor that you might
buy at a local electronics shop

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.320
to put in a circuit.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.600
And this is the kind of capacitor
that they use here at the lab.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:18.940
Each one of these red boxes can store 100,000
volts, and there are 20 of them in this tower.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:23.380
So when the trigger fires, all that power
is going to go through the resistors in front of me,

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:27.900
up, down through that little brown wire
to the single point.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.760
Then it’s going to go through the air, as
lightning, to the ground rod.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.760
There’s just going to be
a drone in the way.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:34.440
Oh, here we go.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:35.540
Here we go.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:47.880
It's charging.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:51.740
We’re at a million.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:52.700
Here we go.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:54.780
Come on...

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.420
Yes!

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.580
I think we got it(!)

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.460
Ohh, look at that!

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.260
Yeah, it’s not even very hot.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.270
That’s actually a little bit disappointing.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:13.599
I was kinda hoping...

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.760
-- Oh, it smells, though. Can you smell that?
-- Does it?

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.260
I probably shouldn’t inhale that, actually.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.420
Burnt motor.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.060
What I would do is...
let me just take the battery out.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.680
Yeah, let’s take the battery out.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:24.500
Battery’s intact.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.159
Battery’s still running.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:27.159
Let’s try switching it back on.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.780
I’m going to stand back, just in case...

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.060
Let go...?

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.740
No. The internals are dead.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:38.040
Battery’s good!

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.700
The entry point is coming through the motors
of the propeller, which is made out of metal.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.549
And it seems to have exited out of a point
which is almost the handles.

00:02:46.549 --> 00:02:48.519
Even though they are made out of plastic,

00:02:48.519 --> 00:02:51.500
there’s still a sharp point which means
they have a high electric field.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.400
So you still will get an exit point, which
is what's happened.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.769
But all that current has gone through the
motors and all the electronics, which has

00:02:58.769 --> 00:02:59.769
pretty much fried it.

00:02:59.769 --> 00:03:00.969
But it seems to have skipped the battery!

00:03:00.969 --> 00:03:02.769
The reason why the battery is protected,

00:03:02.769 --> 00:03:05.489
the battery does have a metal casing around it,
if you kind of think about it.

00:03:05.489 --> 00:03:07.060
So it’s basically a Faraday cage.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:09.879
It’s a Faraday cage, so that has probably
protected the battery,

00:03:09.879 --> 00:03:10.879
Right.

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:14.150
But everything around it has been
fried to bits.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:15.150
All right.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:20.150
What I would recommend is almost having something
sticking out from the top.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.200
Almost like a metal electrode again, so like
a lightning interceptor.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.460
So that attaches to that point, and you can
have an exit point.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.069
We'll try and see if we can put a metal tape
and a protruding part outside,

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:34.820
which will hopefully attract the lightning
so it doesn’t go through anything else.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.020
Let's do it!
That’s why we have a backup drone.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:43.160
A million volts.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.040
All right.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:45.180
Here we go!

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.660
- The propellers!
- The propellers!

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.680
It’s on its feet
and the battery’s still lit.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:58.840
Have you lost communication?

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.320
Yeah. It's off. “Aircraft disconnected”.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.880
All right. Let's bring it in.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.220
Go for it, Tom. You first.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.200
Okay, so it blew the propellers off.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.300
-- Man, they went!
-- They did.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:13.740
I can’t see any visible points...

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.340
On the last one there was
charring on the plastic.

00:04:15.349 --> 00:04:18.269
So we’ve got the picture,
similar to last time,

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:21.130
seems to be attaching to the propeller and
coming out… there you go.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.360
It’s completely missed the lightning rod,
hasn’t it?

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:24.360
Yes.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.300
At the end of the day, this actually will
tell you that lightning can be unpredictable

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.300
at the same time.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.770
And in all fairness, it did hit the propellers
again, and comes out the same point.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.220
What are the streamers?

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:38.650
When you have a high voltage, streamers are
what go up to answer the streamer that's coming

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.660
from the cloud.
-- Right.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.460
When both of those connect, then you get a channel.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.700
-- Should have a look at the slo-mo?
-- Absolutely.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.600
-- There you go.
-- There’s the strike...

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:49.560
And there’s the streamer!

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.860
-- The streamer’s still there.
-- Look at that!

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:55.340
The channel’s already formed,
so the current’s already going through.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:05:00.000
Again, you can see little glowing embers,
the motor that's burned out.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.700
All right, so. Battery back in.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:06.540
No.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.310
No, it’s dead.

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:10.940
So the lesson is, if you’re going to fly
your drone in a lightning storm...

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.340
-- Don’t.
-- Don’t.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.640
-- Thank you!
-- No problem, Tom.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:15.780
-- Thanks, guys!
-- Cheers, Tom.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:18.760
Thank you to everyone at the
University of Manchester’s high-voltage lab.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:22.360
Pull down the description for more about them,
and please, don’t try this at home.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:24.680
If you have a high-voltage laboratory at home.

